  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "14566149",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "14566149"
   },
   "source": [
    "### Predictive tests: does the Claude score forecast future CapEx?\n",
    "\n",
    "We now estimate two OLS regressions, following the spirit of Jha et al. (2025):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\textbf{(1)}\\quad \\text{Future CapEx}_i = a + b\\,(\\text{Claude score}_i) + \\varepsilon_i$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\textbf{(2)}\\quad \\text{Future CapEx}_i = a + b\\,(\\text{Claude score}_i) + c\\,(\\text{Tobin's Q}_i) + \\varepsilon_i$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Model (1) asks whether the late-2023 call signal alone predicts realized fiscal-2024 investment. Model (2) adds **Tobin's Q** — the classic market-based predictor of investment — to ask the sharper question: *does the text carry information **beyond** what the firm's valuation already implies?* If `b` stays positive and meaningful after controlling for Q, the language is adding something.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use `statsmodels` for the full regression diagnostics (coefficients, standard errors, t-stats, R², F-test). The outcome `FUTURE_CAPEX` is set to `capx_to_assets` (FY2024 capex scaled by lagged assets) so firms are comparable; change the one variable below to use `capx_growth`, `capx_to_ppe`, or the raw `capx_2024` level instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Read the diagnostics with the sample in mind.** With n ≈ 8 and two regressors, Model (2) has very few residual degrees of freedom — coefficients are illustrative, not inferential. Watch the *sign and magnitude* of `b`, and how much it moves when Q enters, more than the stars."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "49f3daf9",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "49f3daf9"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import statsmodels.api as sm\n",
    "import statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- choose the \"future CapEx\" outcome (one switch) ----\n",
    "FUTURE_CAPEX = \"capx_to_assets\"   # options: capx_to_assets, capx_growth, capx_to_ppe, capx_2024\n",
    "# --------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "# Build the estimation sample: need the score, the outcome, and Q (for model 2)\n",
    "reg = (table.rename(columns={\"signal\": \"claude_score\"})\n",
    "            [[\"claude_score\", \"tobins_q\", FUTURE_CAPEX]]\n",
    "            .dropna()\n",
    "            .reset_index(drop=True))\n",
    "reg = reg.rename(columns={FUTURE_CAPEX: \"future_capex\"})\n",
    "print(f\"Outcome = {FUTURE_CAPEX}   |   estimation sample n = {len(reg)}\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# ============================================================\n",
    "# Model 1:  future_capex = a + b * claude_score + e\n",
    "# ============================================================\n",
    "m1 = smf.ols(\"future_capex ~ claude_score\", data=reg).fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# ============================================================\n",
    "# Model 2:  future_capex = a + b * claude_score + c * tobins_q + e\n",
    "# ============================================================\n",
    "m2 = smf.ols(\"future_capex ~ claude_score + tobins_q\", data=reg).fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=\"*64)\n",
    "print(\"MODEL 1:  future_capex ~ claude_score\")\n",
    "print(\"=\"*64)\n",
    "print(m1.summary())\n",
    "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*64)\n",
    "print(\"MODEL 2:  future_capex ~ claude_score + tobins_q\")\n",
    "print(\"=\"*64)\n",
    "print(m2.summary())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4452ebd0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Side-by-side comparison and diagnostics\n",
    "\n",
    "The table below puts the two models next to each other so you can see what happens to the Claude-score coefficient when Tobin's Q is added — the key comparison. We also report a few standard regression diagnostics: model fit (R²/adjusted R², F-test), and checks on the residuals (Jarque–Bera for normality, Breusch–Pagan for heteroskedasticity) plus a multicollinearity check (VIF) for Model 2."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2fc212b5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from statsmodels.stats.outliers_influence import variance_inflation_factor\n",
    "from statsmodels.stats.diagnostic import het_breuschpagan\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- coefficient comparison ----\n",
    "def grab(m, name):\n",
    "    if name in m.params.index:\n",
    "        return f\"{m.params[name]:+.4f} (t={m.tvalues[name]:+.2f}, p={m.pvalues[name]:.3f})\"\n",
    "    return \"--\"\n",
    "\n",
    "comp = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"Model 1\": [grab(m1, \"claude_score\"), grab(m1, \"tobins_q\"), grab(m1, \"Intercept\"),\n",
    "                f\"{m1.rsquared:.3f}\", f\"{m1.rsquared_adj:.3f}\",\n",
    "                f\"{m1.fvalue:.2f} (p={m1.f_pvalue:.3f})\", int(m1.nobs)],\n",
    "    \"Model 2\": [grab(m2, \"claude_score\"), grab(m2, \"tobins_q\"), grab(m2, \"Intercept\"),\n",
    "                f\"{m2.rsquared:.3f}\", f\"{m2.rsquared_adj:.3f}\",\n",
    "                f\"{m2.fvalue:.2f} (p={m2.f_pvalue:.3f})\", int(m2.nobs)],\n",
    "}, index=[\"b: Claude score\", \"c: Tobin's Q\", \"a: Intercept\",\n",
    "          \"R-squared\", \"Adj. R-squared\", \"F-test\", \"N\"])\n",
    "print(\"Coefficient comparison (Model 1 vs Model 2)\")\n",
    "print(comp.to_string())\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- diagnostics ----\n",
    "print(\"\\n\" + \"-\"*60)\n",
    "print(\"Diagnostics\")\n",
    "print(\"-\"*60)\n",
    "for name, m in [(\"Model 1\", m1), (\"Model 2\", m2)]:\n",
    "    jb_p = sm.stats.stattools.jarque_bera(m.resid)[1]\n",
    "    try:\n",
    "        bp_p = het_breuschpagan(m.resid, m.model.exog)[1]\n",
    "    except Exception:\n",
    "        bp_p = float(\"nan\")\n",
    "    print(f\"{name}: residual normality (Jarque-Bera p) = {jb_p:.3f} | \"\n",
    "          f\"heteroskedasticity (Breusch-Pagan p) = {bp_p:.3f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# VIF for Model 2 regressors (multicollinearity between score and Q)\n",
    "X = sm.add_constant(reg[[\"claude_score\", \"tobins_q\"]])\n",
    "vifs = {col: variance_inflation_factor(X.values, i)\n",
    "        for i, col in enumerate(X.columns) if col != \"const\"}\n",
    "print(\"\\nModel 2 multicollinearity (VIF, >5-10 is a concern):\")\n",
    "for k, v in vifs.items():\n",
    "    print(f\"  {k}: {v:.2f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- the takeaway ----\n",
    "b1 = m1.params.get(\"claude_score\", float(\"nan\"))\n",
    "b2 = m2.params.get(\"claude_score\", float(\"nan\"))\n",
    "print(\"\\n\" + \"-\"*60)\n",
    "print(f\"Claude-score coefficient: {b1:+.4f} (alone)  ->  {b2:+.4f} (with Q)\")\n",
    "print(\"If b stays positive and similar in size after adding Q, the call\")\n",
    "print(\"signal carries information beyond the firm's market valuation.\")"
   ]
  },
