    "---\n",
    "# Wrap-up: what this exercise is really teaching\n",
    "\n",
    "**The pipeline, not the p-value.** Most empirical classes start with clean data. Here you saw where the data *comes from* — and how many judgment calls (prompt wording, the −2…+2 mapping, fiscal-year alignment, the Q proxy) get baked in long before a regression runs. Each is a *researcher degree of freedom*.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Three lessons to take away:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **LLM extraction is powerful but not neutral.** The prompt is a measurement instrument. Word it differently and you get a different dataset (recall Microsoft's sequential-vs-annual ambiguity).\n",
    "2. **Measurement choices are assumptions.** The ordinal scale assumes equal spacing between categories; collapsing or binarizing can change results. Always test robustness.\n",
    "3. **Respect the sample.** With 8 firms, the table and rank correlation are honest; a starred p-value would be a lie. The *same code* scales to hundreds of firms — at which point the regression becomes meaningful. That scalability is the point.\n",
    "\n",
    "**A reproducibility note:** this exercise originally broke because of a deprecated model string (`...20250514`) and the API-vs-Claude.ai billing confusion. That's a real lesson too — pin your model versions and document your environment, or your code stops working six months later.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Where to take it next\n",
    "- Expand the transcript set to dozens/hundreds of firms and re-run unchanged.\n",
    "- Add firm controls (size, Q, cash flow) in a multivariate regression.\n",
    "- Compare the LLM signal head-to-head against Tobin's Q: does the *text* predict investment **beyond** what the market price already implies?\n"
   ]
